// TEST: OrbIdx Wreath Decompact Then Reduce
// The steer in 208's refusal, run end to end. `reduce` over a wreath pool stays
// refused (the orbit multiplicities are not computed), and the message it
// prints says "decompact(W, 0) first for the logical fold" -- so that sentence
// has to be a working program, not advice pointing at another refusal. It is
// the whole reason the fold refusal carries its own diagnostic instead of the
// generic storage one.
//
// ONE WRINKLE the steer has to survive, and it is the reason this test slices:
// a wreath acts on prod(ri) >= 4 raw axes, so its dense image is rank >= 4,
// and `reduce` takes rank-1 arrays only. The remedy is therefore decompact +
// FIBRE, not decompact + reduce: D(1,1,0) is the dense partial read that peels
// three axes and leaves a rank-1 row, which folds like any other array. Every
// step here is an existing feature -- the only new one is the decompaction.
//
// n = 2 keeps the dense image at 2^4 = 16 cells so the pinned fibre can be read
// straight off D's printed rows.
let A = [1.0, 2.0]
let g = lambda(x, y) where comm(x, y) -> x * y
let S = method_for(A, A) <@> g |> compute
let h = lambda(p, q) where comm(p, q) -> p + q
let W = method_for(S, S) <@> h |> compute
let D = decompact(W, 0)
let fibre = D(1, 1, 0)
let total = reduce(fibre, lambda(a, b) -> a + b)
// S(i,j) = A[i]*A[j] = [[1,2],[2,4]]; W(i,j,k,l) = S(i,j) + S(k,l).
// The (1,1) plane of D is S(1,1) + [1,2,2,4] = 4 + [1,2,2,4] = [5,6,6,8], so
// the k = 0 fibre D(1,1,0) is [5, 6] and its sum is 11. D is rank 4 and
// prints as ONE flat row-major run (the auto-printer's single rank regime;
// the per-plane `D[i][j] = [...]` grid lines these pins used to ride are
// retired), so the D pin is the four planes in (0,0) (0,1) (1,0) (1,1) order.
// EXPECT: S = [1, 2, 4]
// EXPECT: W = [2, 3, 5, 4, 6, 8]
// EXPECT: D = [2, 3, 3, 5, 3, 4, 4, 6, 3, 4, 4, 6, 5, 6, 6, 8]
// EXPECT: fibre = [5, 6]
// EXPECT: total = 11
